Arc T9. The caddy-config-to-os.Chmod shape: tainted data reaches a sink, but
all it does is select WHICH resource the sink acts on. A catalogue row can now
declare that, and such a flow leaves the headline and is DISCLOSED with its own
count. It remains a TRUE POSITIVE on the correctness axis -- WI-gohok's
2026-08-27 ruling is untouched -- and is excluded on USEFULNESS, structurally
and per sink, never by a per-case judgement of how interesting a finding is.

VERIFY_CLAIMS_SCHEMA_VERSION 2.3 -> 2.4.

THE OWNER RULED OPTION (i) WITH TWO BITS. TWO MEASUREMENTS CHANGED HOW IT IS
BUILT, AND BOTH ARE ON THE RECORD RATHER THAN IN A COMMIT MESSAGE ONLY.

(1) SUPPRESSION NEEDS A THIRD CONDITION THE RULING DID NOT NAME. The walk
carries NO argument identity -- re-verified at tip: zero hits for
arg_index|argument_index|arg_pos|param_index|argument_role|which_arg, and
TaintFlowFinding records only the sink primitive/module/symbol. So at a MIXED
sink -- fprintf(stream, fmt, ...), #2 in the reached head -- a finding cannot
be attributed to an argument, and the rule as literally written would suppress
one whose tainted value reached `fmt`. That is the false-all-clear direction,
which this codebase already refuses elsewhere (io_boundary.py:202, "a
disclosure boundary without its shadow is a false-all-clear generator").

Classified the top-20 reached head (476 of 706 mentions, reproducing the
costing's 67.4% exactly, which is the instrument's own control):

    R  no content argument at all      2 sinks   34 mentions    7.1%
    D  naming IS the danger            6 sinks   83 mentions   17.4%
    M  MIXED, names AND takes content  9 sinks  276 mentions   58.0%
    C  no resource-naming argument     3 sinks   83 mentions   17.4%

Owner ruled: ADD THE GUARD. Suppression requires `content: []` -- an EXPLICIT
declaration that the sink takes no content argument. ABSENT and [] are
DIFFERENT: absent is CANNOT-DETERMINE and never suppresses. M rows carry both
bits as recorded data and are disclosed, never suppressed.

(2) A ROW IS NOT A PRIMITIVE, which the costing could not see because it
reasoned about rows abstractly. A catalogue row is a (module, boundary) group
with a LIST of functions, and they do not share a class: c.yaml's stdio row
holds fourteen names spanning ALL FOUR -- fflush is R, fopen/fclose are D,
fprintf is M, printf is C. 11 of the 20 head primitives sit in a row holding a
different class. Splitting those rows would work and is DELIBERATELY NOT DONE:
row ORDER is load-bearing here and the file says so ("THIS ROW STAYS FIRST: an
unstamped call abstains to the first-declared row"), so reshuffling four large
rows to carry an annotation would move abstention behaviour for primitives
unrelated to T9. The annotation is therefore keyed PER FUNCTION inside the row.

THE WHOLE CHAIN SHIPS, NOT THE PRODUCER HALF. Catalogue row -> IoPrimitive ->
TaintSink.resource_naming_only -> TaintFlowFinding -> ClaimVerdict
.resource_naming_flows -> a SENTENCE on the confirmed path: "they are true
flows excluded as not useful, not an absence of flows". A suppression that
appeared only as a JSON key would be a deletion wearing a disclosure's
clothes, and the confirmed path is exactly where a silent filter misleads most.

Verified on the real catalogue, all four behaviours distinct: fflush and
DialTimeout suppress; fprintf does not (the guard); unlink does not (the danger
bit); printf does not (un-annotated). Un-annotated rows behave exactly as
before, so this is forward-only.

THREE TEST FAILURES, ALL MINE, AND ONE WAS A REAL BUG: to_dict dropped
